  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">ILO, 2022-2025, Decent Work and Labour Rights in Southern Africa</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="SV">ILO, 2022-2025, Decent Work and Labour Rights in Southern Africa</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="SV">Det aktuella projektet har arbetande fattiga unga, m&#xe4;n och kvinnor som m&#xe5;lgrupp. Det bygger p&#xe5; ett starkt partnerskap mellan International Labour Organisation (ILO), Southern African Development Community (SADC) och dess medlemsl&#xe4;nder, och viktiga intressenter s&#xe5;som The Southern African Trade Union Coordination Council (SATUCC) och SADC Private Sector Forum (SPSF).
Projektet kommer att arbeta p&#xe5; tv&#xe5; niv&#xe5;er med syftet att st&#xe4;rka SADC-sekretariatets kapacitet att f&#xf6;respr&#xe5;ka uppskalning av systeminsatser; f&#xf6;resl&#xe5; och fr&#xe4;mja effektiva policyf&#xf6;r&#xe4;ndringar i dess medlemsl&#xe4;nder samt att driva p&#xe5; l&#xe5;ngsiktiga och h&#xe5;llbara f&#xf6;rb&#xe4;ttringar f&#xf6;r utsatta unga, kvinnor och m&#xe4;n i byggnadssektorn (sub-regional niv&#xe5;). Dessutom kommer nationella projekt att genomf&#xf6;ras liksom samarbete med nationella regeringar, privata akt&#xf6;rer, fackf&#xf6;reningar och arbetsgivarorganisationer i Mo&#xe7;ambique, Zimbabwe och Sydafrika f&#xf6;r att ta itu med viktiga systembegr&#xe4;nsningar i en sektor d&#xe4;r den stora majoriteten arbetskraft &#xe4;r l&#xe5;gavl&#xf6;nad och d&#xe4;r bristande s&#xe4;kerhet och informell anst&#xe4;llning &#xe4;r r&#xe5;dande. De tre f&#xf6;reslagna l&#xe4;nderna har identifierats och valts ut p&#xe5; grund av deras snabbt v&#xe4;xande byggnadssektorer, underskott i anst&#xe4;ndiga arbetsvillkor liksom p&#xe5; grund av existerande erfarenheter och relationer med nyckelpartners och p&#xe5;g&#xe5;ende relaterade utvecklingsinsatser.</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Targeting the working poor young, men and women, and the micro, small and medium sized enterprises (MSMEs) of the building construction sector that employ a majority of the labour force in the Southern Africa sub-region, the Project builds on an already strong partnership between International Labour Organisation (ILO), Southern African Development Community (SADC) and its member states, and vital stakeholders such as Southern African Trade Union Coordination Council (SATUCC) and SADC Private Sector Forum (SPSF).
Adopting a market systems development approach, the Project will work on two levels
aimed to strengthen the capacity of the SADC Secretariat to advocate for scaling-up
systemic interventions; propose and promote effective policy change across its member
states, and drive long-term and sustainable improvements for vulnerable female and male
labourers in the building-construction sector (sub-regional level); and to implement
national projects and engage with national governments, private sector actors, trade
unions and employers organisations in Mozambique, Zimbabwe and South Africa by
addressing key systemic constraints in the sector in which the vast majority of workers are
locked in low wage, inadequate safety and informal employment (see footnote). The three
suggested countries have been identified and selected due to their growing construction
sectors, decent work deficits as well as experiences and relationships with key partners
and ongoing related interventions.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The project objective is to contribute to improved labour rights in the building-construction sector in the Southern Africa Development Community. The project, which will be implemented using a systemic approach to decent work and will work at the sub-regional level (Outcome 1) as well as in three countries: South Africa, Mozambique and Zimbabwe (Outcome 2). 

The project will aim to improve labour rights through targeting interventions that provide benefit to the working poor and young women and men and the micro, small and medium enterprises (MSMEs) that employ them. These target groups include the workers who are most likely to have few labour rights - including poor wages, occupational safety and health, contract stability and informality  and the MSMEs, who disproportionately struggle to provide decent work to their employees relative to larger contractors. 

As the project will be grounded in a systemic approach to decent work, it will strive to benefit the target groups in long-term sustainable way that can reach scale. It will do this by working through key project partners, including the South African Development Community (SADC), regional and national employers associations and trade unions, SADC member government bodies and the private sector.</narrative>
  </description>
